From 0df7839a19382186136b650a839e7dca84a2e7cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Keith Packard <keithp@keithp.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 05:07:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/20] libos/linux: Report the kernel's limits from sysconf

- _SC_ARG_MAX: fs/exec.c caps arguments at three quarters of _STK_LIM
  (6MiB) and never goes below its ARG_MAX (128KiB). With no stack limit
  sysconf said there was no argument limit at all.
- _SC_CHILD_MAX is RLIMIT_NPROC, not the POSIX minimum.
- _SC_SIGQUEUE_MAX and _SC_TIMER_MAX are RLIMIT_SIGPENDING; each timer
  takes a queued signal when it is created.
- _SC_MQ_OPEN_MAX, _SC_SEM_NSEMS_MAX and _SC_THREAD_THREADS_MAX have no
  limit of their own.
- _SC_SYMLOOP_MAX is the kernel's MAXSYMLINKS, 40.
- _SC_NPROCESSORS_CONF and _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN count the possible and
  online CPUs in /sys, or the affinity mask without it.

The resource limits are read with the raw system call, adding
RLIMIT_NPROC and RLIMIT_SIGPENDING to the Linux numbers, so sysconf
stays async-signal-safe and leaves errno alone.
